<p>Prime Minister Keir Starmer’s Labour Party suffered heavy early losses in UK local and regional elections, while Nigel Farage’s Reform UK gained seats on English councils as votes were counted Friday, May 8. The contests included 5,066 council seats across 136 councils in England, six mayoral races, 129 seats in the Scottish Parliament and 96 seats in the Welsh Senedd. With counting still underway, Labour had lost more than 200 councillors in England and control of seven councils, while Reform had added more than 300 councillors and gained control of one council. Early results showed Labour losing ground in traditional strongholds in central and northern England, including Hartlepool, Chorley, Wigan, Redditch and Tamworth. The vote was described as a test of Starmer’s leadership less than two years after Labour’s July 2024 general election victory. Analysts and Labour lawmakers cited anger over the government’s record, economic strains and political missteps as pressures on Starmer, while Reform, the Greens and Liberal Democrats all gained from voter movement away from Labour and the Conservatives in early results.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/qiNcnjxeo6uumuJjqaO9UrAxo67xSm_Xy3P3pn7QVLlsRmXarFwdOULrGWu3vihshChCUPdugIvP5f8NomrxD9-Ou1awhVyFK2GN8F5SSS3L9pFxT75DaGwMk8zURkeJcRBRfqbfkmLi7u0KsF437F7yGK0xxbIKY8L-y4liM2MnfZRd-vve1QUQea0eyGvTifgIzflno0_AZEQ0MEjbEyY_vUF6mvOgqU4Rv430TBhzlaj8Dg5DZql3BjsdFFB3et5FsNueUGmeYDhLZw' alt='Britain&#x27;s Prime Minister Keir Starmer speaks to Labour Party members and supporters during the launch of Labour&#x27;s local elections campaign at City College in Wolverhampton, England, Monday March 30, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Partial tally: After 39 of 136 councils had reported, Labour was down 246 councillors, Reform had gained 325, the Conservatives were down 119, the Liberal Democrats were up 35 and the Greens were up 26.</li><li>Council shifts: In Hartlepool, Reform won all 12 seats being contested, pushing the previously Labour-held council into no overall control.</li><li>Northern losses: Labour lost control of Tameside for the first time in nearly 50 years after Reform won all 14 seats up for election there.</li><li>Wigan result: Reform candidates won every seat Labour was defending in the former mining hub of Wigan, according to Hum English.</li><li>Next results: PBS reported that polls closed at 10 p.m. on May 7 and that many results were expected on Friday afternoon.</li></ul><blockquote>The picture has been pretty much as bad as anyone expected for Labour, or worse - John Curtice</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Nigel Farage: Farage said Reform’s early performance was “way exceeding” his expectations as his party gained council seats in England. (<a href='https://english.aaj.tv/news/330458614/uks-labour-party-suffers-heavy-early-losses-as-reform-gains-in-elections'>Aaj TV</a>)</li><li>Conservative Party: Party chairman Kevin Hollinrake said the Conservatives expected a difficult set of elections after their 2024 defeat and would keep trying to show voters they had changed. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2026/may/07/elections-2026-local-scotland-wales-reform-green-labour-conservatives-live-news-updates'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Liberal Democrats: The party said voters were disappointed with Labour and opposed to Reform’s politics, and described Liberal Democrat councillors as local candidates focused on services. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2026/may/07/elections-2026-local-scotland-wales-reform-green-labour-conservatives-live-news-updates'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Labour lawmakers: Some Labour lawmakers said Starmer would face renewed pressure to quit or set a timetable to leave if the party also performed poorly in Scotland and Wales. (<a href='https://english.aaj.tv/news/330458614/uks-labour-party-suffers-heavy-early-losses-as-reform-gains-in-elections'>Aaj TV</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href=